  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "8mb40nm",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Introduction to Biological Classification"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Biological classification organizes living organisms into hierarchical groups based on cellular structure, body organization, and nutritional modes.",
        "R.H. Whittaker proposed the Five Kingdom Classification system, dividing organisms into Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, and Animalia.",
        "Carl Linnaeus introduced Binomial Nomenclature, providing every organism a scientific two-part name (Genus and Species), earning him the title 'Father of Taxonomy'.",
        "Kingdom Monera consists entirely of prokaryotes, while Protista, Fungi, Plantae, and Animalia consist of eukaryotes.",
        "Carl Woese proposed the Modern Three Domain System (Archaea, Bacteria, Eukarya) based on ribosomal RNA (16S rRNA) sequences."
      ],
      "id": "tunzsge"
    },
